# PXQU tier map 'pxqu-flashnext-4xp100-150k': Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (arch qwen4exp),
# 48 layers, 512 experts, 10 used. COMPLETE map: 835 rules, one per quantizable tensor.
#
# TARGET  4x Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB (~61.12 GiB usable), -fa on, -c 150016, -b 2048 -ub 2048,
#         -ctk f16 -ctv f16, -wgt 1, n_seq_max 1, per_layer_token_embd pinned to CPU.
# RESULT  GPU-resident weights 49.935 GiB; total footprint 59.08 GiB central /
#         60.48 GiB conservative => 2.04 GiB / 0.64 GiB headroom. FITS.
# FILE    101.085 GiB on disk (51.150 GiB of it host-side: per_layer_token_embd + token_embd).
# EXPERTS 47.051 GiB, avg 3.3458 bpw, hist {pxq3: 132, pxq4: 12}. Floor is pxq3 everywhere:
#         no expert tensor is starved below 3.25 bpw.
#
# WHY EACH FAMILY GOT ITS TIER
#   routed experts (144)  pxq3 floor / pxq4 on 12 tensors. 120.796 B of the 125.1 B
#     on-GPU params live here, so this is the only family with enough mass to close an
#     11.6 GiB gap. pxq4 goes to blk.0 and blk.47 in full (the two boundary blocks: blk.0's
#     MoE writes the residual every later block reads, blk.47's is read by the head) and to
#     ffn_down_exps of blk.1-3 and blk.44-46. down is favoured over gate/up because this
#     architecture has NO ffn_norm and NO output_norm (build_qwen4exp.cpp:8-12) -- the hc
#     mixer is the only normalisation -- so down's error enters the wide residual with
#     nothing downstream to rescale it, and its input is the outlier-heavy silu(gate)*up
#     product. NOTE: the fusion2 sens.json ranks gate above down; that was measured on an
#     arch WITH conventional norms. This ordering is REASONED, not measured -- see RISKS.
#   attn_qkv / attn_gate / attn_q / attn_output / ssm_out / *_shexp   pxq4 (unchanged).
#     1.46 GiB total. BACKBONE_REV2 exists because 4-bit MXFP4 on attn_output measured 3.2x
#     the RMS error of Q4_K_M; these are active on every token and are not where to save.
#     ssm_out moves mxfp4 -> pxq4: identical bytes (287.05 vs 286.88 MiB), better codec
#     (wrel 0.0696 vs mxfp4's coarser E8M0-shared-exponent form).
#   hc_*_down / hc_*_up / output_hc_*   f16 -> q8_0. 1.172 GiB -> 0.623 GiB, the single
#     largest non-expert saving (0.549 GiB). These are the rank-320 mixer projections that
#     ARE this model's layer norm, so they get the highest quantized tier available and are
#     deliberately NOT taken to pxq6 (wrel 0.0343, ~6x q8_0's error) for a further 0.24 GiB.
#   hc_*_inject   f16 kept. [10240,4] = 80 KB each, 7.5 MiB total. Rank-4 injection into the
#     residual; no reason to touch it and PXQ would be demoted anyway (ne1=4 < 64).
#   attn_k / attn_v / ssm_alpha / ssm_beta / indexer.*   q8_0 (unchanged). 72 MiB total.
#     attn_k/v feed the KV cache directly; ssm_alpha/beta are the DeltaNet gates, where a
#     small error compounds through a 36-layer recurrence.
#   ple_key / ple_value   mxfp4 -> q8_0. +16.6 MiB. Layer 1 only. This is the read head on
#     the 50.7 GiB n-gram gather table; the whole PLE contribution passes through these two
#     matrices once, so 16 MiB buys cheap insurance on a path with no redundancy.
#   output.weight   q8_0 -> q6_K. 644.14 -> 497.31 MiB. 6.5625 bpw on a 248,320-row head is
#     the standard safe point; not taken to pxq4 (would save a further 175 MiB) because a
#     2560-wide head over a 248k vocab has very little redundancy per row.
#   token_embd.weight   q6_K (unchanged), and DELIBERATELY NOT a panel codec -- see RISKS.
#   per_layer_token_embd.weight   q8_0. Rule is documentation-only: the write loop copies
#     this tensor through untouched (llama-quantize.cpp:2047-2058). Stays on CPU via
#     -ot 'per_layer_token_embd\.weight=CPU'; block-32 codec, 50.664 GiB of host RAM.
#   norms / ssm_a / ssm_dt.bias / ssm_conv1d / ple_conv1d / ffn_gate_inp   NO RULE, by design.
#     ssm_conv1d and ple_conv1d are [4, 10240]: ne0=4, so the write loop's
#     `quantize &= (ne[0] % 32 == 0)` copies them through as f32. Naming a codec here would
#     be inert at best; every block-32 codec asserts n_per_row % 32 == 0 and ABORTS.
#     ffn_gate_inp.weight (the 512-way router, 240 MiB f32) is excluded by name and cannot
#     be retiered from a .tiers file at all. All *_norm.weight and every 1-D tensor are
#     likewise excluded.
#
# DELIBERATELY STARVED, AND THE RISK
#   1. 132 of 144 expert tensors sit at pxq3 (3.25 bpw, wrel 0.1435 vs pxq4's 0.0696 --
#      roughly 2x the weight error). Layers 4-43 have NO pxq4 tensor at all. With 512 experts
#      at top-10 each expert sees ~1/51 of tokens, so per-expert outliers are not averaged
#      away by the router. This is the main quality cost of the recipe.
#   2. The gate/up/down ordering is reasoned from the missing-norm argument above, not
#      measured. No sens.json exists for this arch and the shipped GGUF carries no imatrix
#      (no quantize.imatrix.* KVs). If a real sensitivity sweep lands and ranks gate first,
#      move the 12 pxq4 slots without changing any byte count -- an upgrade is exactly
#      100.00 MiB on gate/up AND on down, so the map re-solves inside the same budget.
#   3. hc_* at q8_0: near-lossless individually, but the error has no downstream norm to
#      absorb it and it compounds across 96 mixers. Gate on same-top-token, not on PPL alone.
#   4. output at q6_K: the one change on the path that turns hidden states into logits.
#
# BUILD (CPU job, hours; run from the repo root; flags MUST precede the positionals)
#   PXA_PXQ_BACKBONE=v2,universal PXA_PXQ_COMPOSITION_OVERRIDE=1 \
#   ./build/bin/llama-quantize --allow-requantize \
#     --imatrix /path/to/flashnext.imatrix \
#     --pxq-universal /path/to/work-tree/pxa-bench/pxq-universal/recipes/pxqu-flashnext-4xp100-150k.tiers \
#     /path/to/models/qwen4exp/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-BF16-pleq8.gguf \
#     /path/to/models/qwen4exp/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-PXQU-150k.gguf PXQ_UNIVERSAL 32
#   PXA_PXQ_COMPOSITION_OVERRIDE=1 IS MANDATORY: PXQ-family share is 47.96%, below the 50%
#   assertion, because 51.15 GiB of the file is gather tables that may never be a panel
#   codec. Without it the run deletes its own output after hours of work.
#   Quantize from the BF16 source, never from the shipped PXQ4 file (double quantization).
#
# POST-BUILD CHECKS (all from the run's own stdout)
#   1. "--pxq-universal: 835 tier rules from ..."
#   2. zero "CUSTOM-Q DEMOTED" lines
#   3. output composition shows NO mxfp4 (an unmatched tensor under PXQ_UNIVERSAL falls to
#      MXFP4 silently -- pxqu_out is absent from the ftype-promotion list at
#      llama-quantize.cpp:2399-2403)
#   4. PXQ-family share printed as ~47.96%
#
# Consumed by llama-quantize --pxq-universal. Format: <ECMAScript regex>=<ggml type>,
# one per line, first match wins, no whitespace and no commas anywhere.
